{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"auto-const-constexpr","title":"auto, const & constexpr","group":"Language Basics","order":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>auto</code> lets the compiler deduce a type; <code>const</code> promises a value won't change; <code>constexpr</code> asks for it to be computed at compile time.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i><code>auto</code> type deduction</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;map&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    auto n = 42;              // int\n    auto pi = 3.14;           // double\n    auto s = std::string(\"hi\");\n    std::map&lt;std::string, std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; index{{\"a\", {1, 2}}};\n    for (const auto&amp; [key, list] : index)   // instead of spelling out the pair type\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; key &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; list.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; pi &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a: 2\n42 3.14 hi</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Deduced as</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>auto x = ci;</code> (where <code>const int ci</code>)</td><td><code>int</code></td><td>Top-level <code>const</code> and references are dropped — you get a copy</td></tr><tr><td><code>auto&amp; r = ci;</code></td><td><code>const int&amp;</code></td><td>A reference keeps <code>const</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>const auto&amp; r = expr;</code></td><td><code>const T&amp;</code></td><td>Read-only view, no copy — the default in range-<code>for</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>auto* p = &amp;x;</code></td><td><code>int*</code></td><td>Documents that it's a pointer</td></tr><tr><td><code>auto&amp;&amp; r = expr;</code></td><td><code>T&amp;</code> or <code>T&amp;&amp;</code></td><td>Forwarding reference — binds to anything</td></tr><tr><td><code>auto v = {1, 2};</code></td><td><code>std::initializer_list&lt;int&gt;</code></td><td>Surprise! Braces with <code>=</code> deduce an initializer list</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>for (auto x : bigStrings)</code> copies every element. Use <code>const auto&amp;</code> to read and <code>auto&amp;</code> to modify.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i><code>const</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    const int maxUsers = 100;       // must be initialised; can't be changed later\n    int value = 5;\n    const int* p1 = &amp;value;         // pointer to const int: *p1 is read-only\n    int* const p2 = &amp;value;         // const pointer: p2 can't be re-seated\n    *p2 = 6;                        // OK — the int itself isn't const\n    p1 = &amp;maxUsers;                 // OK — p1 itself isn't const\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *p1 &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; value &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>100 6</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Reading trick</div><p>Read pointer declarations <strong>right to left</strong>: <code>const int* p</code> → “p is a pointer to an int that is const”; <code>int* const p</code> → “p is a const pointer to int”. <code>const int* const p</code> → both.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>int main() {\n    const int limit = 10;\n    limit = 20;           // error: cannot assign to a const variable\n    return limit;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i><code>constexpr</code> — compile-time values &amp; functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nconstexpr long long factorial(int n) {       // may run at compile time\n    return n &lt;= 1 ? 1 : n * factorial(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    constexpr auto f10 = factorial(10);      // computed by the compiler\n    static_assert(factorial(5) == 120);      // checked at compile time\n    std::array&lt;int, factorial(4)&gt; arr{};     // usable as an array size\n    int n = 6;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; f10 &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; arr.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; factorial(n) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // also callable at run time\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3628800 24 720</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>const</code></td><td>Can't be modified after initialisation. Value may be known only at run time.</td></tr><tr><td><code>constexpr</code> variable</td><td>Must be a compile-time constant (implies <code>const</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><code>constexpr</code> function</td><td><em>Can</em> be evaluated at compile time if its arguments are constants; otherwise runs normally.</td></tr><tr><td><code>consteval</code> function (C++20)</td><td><em>Must</em> be evaluated at compile time (an “immediate function”).</td></tr><tr><td><code>constinit</code> (C++20)</td><td>A static variable must be initialised at compile time, but can change later.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nconsteval int square(int x) { return x * x; }\nint main() {\n    constexpr int s = square(9);   // fine: argument is a constant\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // int n = 3; square(n);       // error: n is not a constant expression\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>81</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i><code>const</code> everywhere it applies</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Make variables, parameters (<code>const std::string&amp;</code>) and member functions <code>const</code> whenever they don't mutate — the compiler then enforces your intent.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Using <code>#define MAX 100</code> for constants — macros ignore scope and types; use <code>constexpr int kMax = 100;</code></p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between const and constexpr?</summary><div><p><code>const</code> means <em>read-only after initialisation</em>; the initial value may come from run-time input. <code>constexpr</code> means the value is a <em>compile-time constant</em> (or, for functions, that they can be evaluated at compile time). Every <code>constexpr</code> variable is <code>const</code>, but not the other way round.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does auto make C++ dynamically typed?</summary><div><p>No. The type is deduced once, at compile time, from the initialiser, and never changes. <code>auto x = 1; x = \"hi\";</code> is a compile error.</p></div></details></section>"}